{"t":1789866435,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"88361819-4b95-4b4d-962e-3262c953cc16","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-09-20T00:20:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 200020\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n820 PM EDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nAdded some light sprinkles in across western and central MD this \nevening. Additional scattered showers early Sunday morning and again \nSunday afternoon as a front slides through. Small Craft\nAdvisories expanded.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Scattered showers and perhaps a few thunderstorms look to \nfinish out the weekend as a front passes through. \n\n- 2) Daily shower chances and cooler temperatures persists through \nmidweek with drier conditions for the upcoming weekend. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Scattered showers and perhaps a few thunderstorms look to \nfinish out the weekend as a front passes through. \n\nAdded some light showers and sprinkles in across central and western \nMD this evening as spotty activity drops south from central and \nwestern PA. Outside of these showers and sprinkles, is increased low \nand mid level cloudiness. This is in association with a squeeze play \nbetween fronts with moisture advection increasing from the south and \neast and a cold front slowly dropping down from the Great Lakes \nregion. Low and mid level clouds will remain abundant overnight with \nsome filtered breaks the further south you get toward the I-64 \ncorridor. Most will remain dry overnight, but with moisture \nadvection increasing over a shallow cool layer aloft expect humidity \nvalues to gradually climb and spotty showers likely developing over \nthe mountains heading into Sunday morning. This shower activity will \nbe scattered in nature and in association with the warm front and \nlead piece of shortwave energy ahead of the front that is currently \nlocated back across the eastern Great Lakes and Ohio River Valley. \nThe first round of showers should exit the area around midday with a \nsecondary area of showers and perhaps a few thunderstorms working \nthrough the region Sunday afternoon and evening as the cold front \ndrops through. Current hi-res guidance has slowed the front up a bit \nwhile dissipating/washing it out over the region Sunday evening into \nSunday night. Deterministic synoptic guidance has a similar \nsolution while keeping a ripple of low pressure riding along the\nboundary Sunday evening into Sunday night forcing it south. \nWith that said, severe weather does not appear to be a concern \nalthough a few thunderstorm cannot be ruled out over central VA,\nsouthern MD, and the northern neck of VA where some breaks of \nsun may filter through. Any of these storms will be capable of \nlocally gusty winds, heavy rainfall, and lightning. \n\nThe front will eventually shunt southward heading into Monday \nmorning although on and off shower activity will persists into \nthe new workweek given increased onshore flow. Expect a \nnoticeable cooling trend beginning Monday in the wake of the \ncold front late Sunday night. Highs Sunday will push into the \nupper 70s and low 80s (low 70s mountains) with overnight lows in\nthe upper 50s and mid 60s. Highs Monday will struggle to get \nout of the upper 60s and low 70s as more of a wedging pattern \nsets up with increasing onshore flow. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Daily shower chances and cooler temperatures persists through \nmidweek with drier conditions for the upcoming weekend. \n\nMid-level moisture overhead will coincide with an area of low \npressure just off the East Coast tracking north on Wednesday. Weak \ninstability at the surface will suppress thunderstorm activity for \nthe most part, but expect rain showers to continue through Wednesday \nand Thursday as shortwave disturbances track across the Mid- \nAtlantic. High temperatures continue to range in the 60s as \nthese disturbances move through. \n\nAs the coastal low skirts the Mid-Atlantic coastline, winds will \nbe elevated Wednesday and Thursday with wind gusts between 20-30 MPH \npossible, especially along the Chesapeake Bay. Winds could \npotentially be higher over the waters, and this onshore flow \ncombined with stronger tidal influence could increase coastal flood \nrisk during this period. Refer to the coastal flooding section below \nfor more details. \n\nAs this system tracks north towards the end of the week, winds along \nthe coastline should gradually decrease. Mid-level dry air moves in \noverhead from the Midwest, which should lead to diminishing rainfall \npotential heading into the weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /00Z SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nVFR conditions look to continue through at least 02z/10pm with \nMVFR cigs settling in overnight as low and mid level clouds \nthicken. Low and mid level clouds will persists through Sunday \nafternoon as a result of increased moisture advection ahead of a\nseries of fronts pushing through. Most locations will remain \ndry tonight outside of a passing shower or sprinkle for \nterminals west of MRB. Shower coverage will increase early to \nmid Sunday morning between 10- 16z/6am-12pm as a result of a \nwarm front lifting into the region. Moisture advection with this\nfront combined with a leading piece of shortwave energy will \nyield scattered light to moderate showers mainly at terminals \nnorth of KCHO. Expect a lull in the showers midday with an \nadditional round of scattered showers and perhaps a few \nthunderstorms Sunday afternoon beyond 19z-23z/3-7pm. Greatest \nopportunity for a thunderstorm would be south of the corridor \nfrom DCA down to CHO/RIC where breaks of sun will be more \nprevalent. Severe weather is not expected although locally gusty\nwinds and brief heavy rain cannot be ruled out with any \nthunderstorms that form. CIGS become more scattered to broken \nSunday evening into Sunday night before restrictions return \nMonday as onshore east to northeast flow increases. \n\nExpect a mashup of MVFR to perhaps periods of IFR cigs on both \nMonday and Tuesday as a result of increased onshore flow. Low and \nmid level cloudiness will be persistent through midweek along with \ndaily chances for showers. Sub-VFR conditions could persist through \nThursday as scattered showers continue throughout the region. Gusty \nwinds between 20-25 knots are possible on Thursday. Winds flow from \nnortheasterly to northerly by Friday. \n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nSmall Craft Advisories have been expanded to encompass not only the \nopen waters of the bay and tidal Potomac, but the middle waters of \nbay north toward the Baltimore Inner Harbor. SCA level winds will \ncontinue on and off through Sunday midday as onshore flow increases \nand the gradient tightens ahead of the incumbent frontal boundaries. \nAdditional SCA level conditions may be needed for the waters late \nSunday afternoon and evening and again early next week. \n\nNorth to northeasterly wind gusts remain elevated through Wednesday \nand Thursday as an area of low pressure remains off the East Coast. \nShowers & a couple of thunderstorms are possible over the waters \nduring this period. As this system begins to track north late in the \nweek, winds should begin to decrease heading into the weekend. \n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nCoastal Flood Advisory has been issued for Anne Arundel County.\nCBOFS guidance seems like the only guidance performing well with\nthe elevated water levels and showing the rapid response due to\nthe onshore flow. \n\nOnshore flow will persist through at least the middle of next\nweek with water levels remaining elevated with minor coastal\nflooding expected at times of high tide. At this time, the risk\nof moderate coastal flooding seems low.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...Coastal Flood Advisory until 5 AM EDT Sunday for MDZ014.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...Small Craft Advisory until 2 AM EDT Sunday for ANZ531>534-\n     537>543.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...EST/SRT\nAVIATION...EST/SRT\nMARINE...EST/SRT\n"},"forecast":{"t":1789866421,"days":{"2026-09-19":{"hi":null,"lo":69,"pop":7,"short":"Mostly Cloudy"},"2026-09-20":{"hi":85,"lo":65,"pop":64,"short":"Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-09-21":{"hi":75,"lo":62,"pop":45,"short":"Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-09-22":{"hi":69,"lo":58,"pop":53,"short":"Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-09-23":{"hi":67,"lo":58,"pop":47,"short":"Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-09-24":{"hi":67,"lo":55,"pop":31,"short":"Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-09-25":{"hi":68,"lo":55,"pop":26,"short":"Chance Rain Showers"},"2026-09-26":{"hi":72,"lo":null,"pop":15,"short":"Slight Chance Rain Showers"}}},"grid":{"t0":1789866000,"n":48,"ceil":[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null],"vis":[10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,10.0,4.0,4.0,4.0,4.0,4.0,4.0,4.0,4.0,4.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,5.0,1.0],"spd":[9,9,9,8,8,7,7,7,7,7,8,8,9,10,10,10,10,10,9,9,8,6,5,5,5,5,4,4,4,4,5,7,8,8,9,9,9,10,10,10,10,9,10,11,11,11,10,9],"gst":[12,12,12,11,11,10,10,10,10,11,11,12,13,15,15,15,15,14,14,13,11,9,7,6,6,7,6,6,7,7,8,9,10,11,12,11,13,14,14,13,14,13,14,15,14,14,13,10],"dir":[120,130,130,140,140,150,150,160,160,160,170,170,180,190,190,190,190,190,190,190,190,200,190,200,200,230,250,270,310,330,340,0,0,10,10,10,20,30,30,30,40,40,40,50,50,50,50,50],"pop":[5,5,5,6,6,6,5,8,5,6,6,16,25,34,26,25,22,33,33,33,33,33,33,66,66,66,66,66,66,41,41,41,41,41,41,15,15,15,15,15,15,26,26,26,26,26,26,42],"temp":[22.8,21.7,21.1,21.1,21.1,21.1,21.1,21.1,21.7,21.1,21.1,21.7,22.8,24.4,25,26.7,27.8,27.8,27.8,27.8,28.3,27.2,26.7,25.6,23.3